    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This project explored multiple approaches for multiple-choice question answering, progressing from traditional baselines to deep learning, transformer-based, retrieval-augmented, and ensemble methods.\n",
    "\n",
    "The baseline approaches provided useful reference points, with the Majority Baseline achieving a validation MAP@3 of 0.4200, TF-IDF Cosine Similarity 0.2955, Sentence Transformer 0.4155, and Zero-Shot BART-MNLI 0.5471. More advanced approaches produced substantial improvements. The RAG pipeline achieved a MAP@3 of 0.8850, while the fine-tuned DistilBERT model achieved 0.9312.\n",
    "\n",
    "Among the individual models, the BiLSTM built from scratch produced the strongest validation performance, achieving a MAP@3 of 0.9931. The Weighted Ensemble also performed strongly with a MAP@3 of 0.9875, although it did not improve upon the BiLSTM on the validation set.\n",
    "\n",
    "To ensure that the model comparison was reliable, duplicate MCQs were removed before splitting the data and the final training and validation sets were verified to have zero exact prompt overlap and zero exact full-MCQ overlap. On the common 5-class MCQ evaluation set, BiLSTM achieved the highest Accuracy and Macro F1, followed by DistilBERT and the RAG pipeline. Error analysis further showed that the models make different types of mistakes, demonstrating differences in their learned representations and prediction strategies.\n",
    "\n",
    "Training behaviour for the trainable models was tracked using Weights & Biases (W&B), including the progression of training and validation loss across epochs. This provides a clearer view of model convergence while keeping the notebook evaluation output focused on final metrics such as Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 and MAP@3.\n",
    "\n",
    "On the Kaggle public leaderboard, the BiLSTM achieved a score of **0.75935**, while the Weighted Ensemble achieved **0.75685**. Therefore, although the ensemble combined information from multiple models, the BiLSTM remained the best-performing final submission. Both scores exceeded the required Kaggle cutoff of 0.73.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the experiments demonstrate that increasing model complexity does not necessarily guarantee better performance. In this dataset, the BiLSTM provided the strongest combination of validation performance and Kaggle leaderboard performance, while the transformer, RAG and ensemble experiments provided useful comparisons and demonstrated the strengths and limitations of different NLP modelling approaches."
